# CHANGELOG

## 20260807

0.7.0: Take a Changes section carrying no marker as one entry.

1. + begin_entry(): Creates an entry and appends it to the list, both the marker branch and the item branch now needing to.
2. ~ parse_changes(): An item met before any marker begins an entry of its own, rather than being dropped by the "&& current_entry" guard, which is gone. A marker tells one version's items from another's within a cumulative section, so a section covering a single version needs none and a good many carry none: jira's 0.2.0 and 0.3.0, startor's first entry, should2expect's whole section. They were reported as not understood at best, and lost at worst.
3. ~ parse_changes(): Lines met before any entry began attach to the first of them, so that they surface when that entry is the one returned. Without this the orphans went missing the moment the items began an entry of their own, which is the thing 0.6.0 exists to prevent: should2expect's "0: Handle verbs ending in y." went again without a word.
4. ~ README.md: Say that a section covering a single version carries no marker.


## 20260807

0.6.0: Mark what the Changes section holds and cannot be parsed, rather than dropping it.

1. + unparsed_items(): A warning on stderr naming the revision and the line, and an item reading "? <line>" in the entry, for whatever the section holds which is none of a marker, a numbered item or a blank line. It follows heading_date(), which does the same for a missing date: warn, leave an obvious hole, and let the run finish rather than stopping it.
2. ~ parse_changes(): + an else, so that a line matching no branch is collected rather than falling through the loop unremarked. + @unparsed_lines.
3. ~ process_initial_version() and process_subsequent_version(): Append the marks to the items. In the latter the collection is taken before changes_are_new?(), which parses the previous version and would otherwise leave that one's lines standing in place of this one's.
4. + UNPARSED_MARK
5. The three shapes which prompted this, none of them worth a branch of its own: exitor's sub-items numbered i, ii and iii; should2expect's marker written "0:" rather than "-/0"; startor's summary written with no marker above it. Eleven lines across the three, every one lost without a word. Worse than the count suggests: should2expect's marker not being recognised left current_entry nil, so the guard on the item branch dropped its three items too, the section came back empty, and the version silently took a structural diff in place of the entry sitting in its header.
6. The lines attach to the entry current when they are met, and orphans, met before any marker, to the run. The Changes section is cumulative across a minor series, so a version's header carries the earlier markers' lines as well as its own, and holding them all against the returned entry put exitor's 0.2.0 sub-items into the entries for 0.2.1 and 0.2.2 too, three versions reporting the same three lines.
7. ~ README.md: Say what happens to a line which cannot be parsed.


## 20260807

0.5.2: Take the parentheses off a marker's summary.

1. + marker_summary(): Strip a wrapping pair of parentheses, and return nil where nothing is left. The older headers write the summary as "-/0 (Text.)" and the newer ones as "-/0: Text.", and the parentheses are the marker's punctuation rather than part of what it says. exitor's entries were coming out as "0.2.2: (Detect full country names correctly.)" against a commit message of the same line without them.
2. ~ parse_changes(): Use marker_summary(), which also absorbs the empty-summary check that followed the assignment.


## 20260807

0.5.1: End the Changes section at any heading, not at one of four named ones.

1. ~ parse_changes(): /(Discussion|Notes|Todo|Examples):/[A-Z][A-Za-z ]*:/, anchored to the end of the line so as it matches a heading and not an item which happens to contain a colon. Markers, numbered items and blank lines are all matched before this branch is reached, so the only lines it can see are headings.
2. The four named sections were a closed list against an open set. gem-homepage's header says "Note:", singular, and so was not ended: its numbered line was taken as an item and written into the entry for 0.2.0. Usage: and Description: appear in other repositories and would have done the same.
3. ~ README.md: Say that any further heading ends the section, rather than naming four.


## 20260807

0.5.0: Report a change of the program's name, which the structural diff could not see.

1. + parse_name(): The program's name from the header, told from the date and the version by their leading digit. It mirrors parse_version() and parse_date(), which already read the same block for the two fields below it.
2. + name_change(): /old/new/ where the two differ, in the notation the changelogs already use, and first among the items, a change of identity leading whatever else the version did. Found while backfilling git-checkout-new-issue, whose 0.1.0 renamed the program from git-checkout_with_jira and left no other trace: the file was already bin/git-checkout-new-issue, so only the header changed and tier 2 reported nothing at all of it.
3. ~ extract_structure(): + name. A name declared in the header is a declaration as much as a require or a def is, and the structure hash is what tier 2 compares.
4. ~ structural_diff(): Begin the items with name_change().
5. ~ empty_structure(): + name, so that a file which is not there compares as having no name rather than as having none of a key.
6. inventory_items() is left alone, so tier 3 is unchanged. A first version does not change its name, and reporting it there would say only that the program is called what it is called.
7. ~ README.md and the Tiers section of the header: Say that tier 2 reports a change of name.


## 20260806

0.4.1: Document the Changes section which tier 1 reads.

1. ~ README.md: + The Changes section, being cumulative across one minor series and restarted at each new one so that it cannot grow without bound, what "Changes since 0.5:" names, what the -/0 and n/n+1 markers mean and why the dash, that items are numbered continuously across the whole section, that only the last marker's items become the entry, and what ends the section. It was the least recoverable part of the format, being visible in the headers but nowhere stated.
2. ~ README.md: Name the tiers where they are described. The script header calls them tiers, and the README twice refers to them by number, but the description itself never introduced the term.


## 20260806

0.4.0: Write the CHANGELOG in the form the repositories already use.

1. ~ format_entry(): A "## <date>" heading, with the version and summary on the first line of the body and the items unindented, in place of a "<version> - <date>" heading with indented items. git-import-all reads this directly, so the output no longer wants converting by hand before an import.
2. ~ write_changelog(): Two blank lines between entries rather than one, so that the boundaries stay visible where items run to several sentences.
3. + heading_date(): Warn on stderr naming the revision, and write ???????? where the script header carries no date, rather than inventing a date or stopping the run. The output is a seed to be edited, so an obvious hole in it is honest.
4. + summary_line()
5. - version_label(): The -/0 and n/n+1 markers exist because a Changes section spans a whole minor series, restarted at each one to keep it from growing without bound. Once every version has its own dated entry there is nothing left for them to disambiguate. They are still parsed out of the headers, merely no longer written.


## 20260806

0.3.0: Add a README and a TODO, and retire the Changes section from the script header.

1. + README.md: What the tool is for, the three tiers and the order they are tried in, the skip on an existing CHANGELOG, the output layout, the relationship to git-import-all and git-boot, and a note on this repository's own CHANGELOG having been seeded by the tool.
2. + TODO.txt: The four items which had been kept as a loose "changelogger roadmap" file alongside the revisions, where git-import-all would have taken it for a revision of its own had it not begun ignoring everything but numbered directories.
3. - The Changes section from the script header. The CHANGELOG holds that record now, and keeping both invites them to disagree. Revisions 0 to 2 keep theirs, being the source the CHANGELOG was seeded from.


